<scenario xmlns="http://www.github/cliffe/SecGen/scenario"
          xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
          xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.github/cliffe/SecGen/scenario">

  <name>Mission 3: Ghost in the Machine - Zero Day Training Network</name>
  <author>Z. Cliffe Schreuders</author>
  <description>
# Mission 3: Ghost in the Machine

Zero Day Syndicate sold Ghost the way into St. Catherine's. The trail leads to WhiteHat Security, a respectable pentest firm with Zero Day's exploit market running out of its back rooms. This is their isolated training network - the box they rehearse exploits on before they sell them.

## Your Objectives

1. **Map the training network** - Sweep the lab and read the services it exposes
2. **Pull the FTP intelligence** - The FTP box runs the trojaned ProFTPD build they sold Ghost
3. **Decode the pricing intel** - The web host serves their price list, only encoded
4. **Exploit the legacy distcc service** - The operational logs that name Ghost and St. Catherine's

## Technical Challenge Path

- Enumerate the network and read the service banners (recon): the FTP login banner and the web host's page source
- Exploit the ProFTPD 1.3.3c distribution source backdoor (compromised 2010 release; no CVE assigned; Metasploit unix/ftp/proftpd_133c_backdoor) for unauthenticated remote root
- Pull and decode the ROT13-encoded exploit price list from the web host (encoding is not encryption)
- Exploit the distcc daemon command execution (CVE-2004-2687, high port 3632; Metasploit unix/misc/distcc_exec) to recover the transaction log

## Narrative Payoff

The transaction log itemises invoice ZDS-2024-0847 to GHOST (Ransomware Incorporated) line by line, the same lines as the buyer's copy recovered at the hospital, then logs the deployment against St. Catherine's Regional Medical Centre. Sable approved it. It is the seller's own ledger to set beside the buyer's invoice.
  </description>

  <type>ctf</type>
  <type>attack-ctf</type>
  <type>pwn-ctf</type>
  <difficulty>medium</difficulty>

  <!-- ftp / distcc root -->
  <CyBOK KA="MAT" topic="Attacks and exploitation">
    <keyword>EXPLOITATION</keyword>
    <keyword>EXPLOITATION FRAMEWORKS</keyword>
    <keyword>BACKDOOR TROJANS</keyword>
  </CyBOK>
  <CyBOK KA="SS" topic="Categories of Vulnerabilities">
    <keyword>CVEs and CWEs</keyword>
  </CyBOK>
  <CyBOK KA="SOIM" topic="PENETRATION TESTING">
    <keyword>PENETRATION TESTING - SOFTWARE TOOLS</keyword>
    <keyword>PENETRATION TESTING - ACTIVE PENETRATION</keyword>
  </CyBOK>
  <CyBOK KA="NS" topic="PENETRATION TESTING">
    <keyword>FILE - TRANSFER PROTOCOL (FTP)</keyword>
  </CyBOK>

  <!-- encoding challenge -->
  <CyBOK KA="F" topic="Artifact Analysis">
    <keyword>Encoding and alternative data formats</keyword>
  </CyBOK>


  <system>
    <system_name>attack_vm</system_name>
    <base distro="Kali" name="MSF"/>

    <input into_datastore="IP_addresses">
      <!-- 0 attack_vm -->
      <value>192.168.100.2</value>
      <!-- 1 zero_day_training_box -->
      <value>192.168.100.3</value>
    </input>

    <utility module_path=".*/parameterised_accounts">
      <input into="accounts">
        <value>{"username":"kali","password":"kali","super_user":"true","strings_to_leak":[],"leaked_filenames":[]}</value>
      </input>
    </utility>

    <utility module_path=".*/iceweasel">
      <input into="accounts">
        <value>{"username":"kali","password":"kali","super_user":"true","strings_to_leak":[],"leaked_filenames":[]}</value>
      </input>
      <input into="autostart">
        <value>false</value>
      </input>
    </utility>

    <utility module_path=".*/kali_top10"/>
    <utility module_path=".*/kali_web"/>

    <network type="private_network">
      <input into="IP_address">
        <datastore access="0">IP_addresses</datastore>
      </input>
    </network>
    <input into_datastore="spoiler_admin_pass">
      <generator type="strong_password_generator"/>
    </input>
    <build type="cleanup">
      <input into="root_password">
        <datastore>spoiler_admin_pass</datastore>
      </input>
    </build>
  </system>

  <system>
    <system_name>ghost_in_machine_vm_network</system_name>
    <base distro="Debian 12" type="server"/>

    <!-- flag 1: network scan. Generated ONCE here (first flag on this system) and
         shown in two service banners below: the FTP login banner and the web
         index source. One generator, so the station still gets exactly four flags. -->
    <input into_datastore="scan_flag">
      <generator type="flag_generator"/>
    </input>

    <input into_datastore="server_root_password">
      <generator type="strong_password_generator"/>
    </input>

    <utility module_path=".*/parameterised_accounts">
      <input into="accounts">
        <value>{"username":"student","password":"student","super_user":"false","strings_to_leak":[],"leaked_filenames":[]}</value>
      </input>
    </utility>
    <utility module_path=".*/handy_cli_tools"/>

    <!-- flag 2: FTP intelligence, via the ProFTPD 1.3.3c source backdoor -> root.
         welcome_msg (AccessGrantMsg, shown on an anonymous login) carries flag1.
         The anonymous pre-leak file carries the service list only (no flag), in
         line with the runbook's "nothing that matters lives in the anonymous folder".
         The post-exploit root leak carries flag2 + the FTP intel string. -->
    <vulnerability module_path="vulnerabilities/unix/ftp/proftpd_133c_backdoor">
      <input into="port">
        <value>21</value>
      </input>
      <input into="welcome_msg">
        <!-- Single line: the module writes it into a quoted AccessGrantMsg. -->
        <encoder module_path="modules/encoders/selector/concatenate">
          <input into="delim">
            <!-- "=" not a space: a bare-space value risks being lost on the encoder command line -->
            <value>=</value>
          </input>
          <input into="strings_to_encode">
            <value>Zero Day training lab. Anonymous access is read only. Lab check-in token</value>
            <datastore>scan_flag</datastore>
          </input>
        </encoder>
      </input>
      <input into="strings_to_pre_leak">
        <!-- Anonymous folder: service list only, no flag -->
        <value>ZERO DAY SYNDICATE - TRAINING LAB (isolated)
=========================================

Services on this network:
- FTP  : ProFTPD 1.3.3c (trojaned 2010 build) - anonymous read
- HTTP : price list (confidential - staff only)
- LEGACY BUILD: distcc on a high port - DO NOT TOUCH after hours

Students: recon first. Everything here answers a scan.
Nothing that matters lives in the anonymous folder.</value>
      </input>
      <input into="strings_to_leak">
        <!-- Post-exploit (root): FTP intel string + flag2 -->
        <value>ZERO DAY SYNDICATE - FTP BOX NOTES (root only)
=============================================

This is the exact build we shipped to GHOST: ProFTPD 1.3.3c, the
compromised source release from 2010. We rehearse the St. Catherine's
delivery on this box before every healthcare job.

Reference: compromised distribution source backdoor, 2010 (no CVE assigned;
Metasploit unix/ftp/proftpd_133c_backdoor). Unauthenticated remote root.

The operational records are not here. They sit on the legacy distcc box.</value>
        <generator type="flag_generator"/>
      </input>
    </vulnerability>

    <!-- flag 3: pricing intel, ROT13-encoded price list served over HTTP (HTML
         comments in the index page source). The public price list: lore_fragment_2's
         tiers and prices without the buyers or reserved stock (P3-2). -->
    <service module_path=".*/http/parameterised_website">
      <input into="strings_to_leak">
        <!-- Plain HTML comment: the web host's banner, carrying the SAME flag1
             value as the FTP login banner (datastore, not a second generator). -->
        <encoder module_path="modules/encoders/selector/concatenate">
          <input into="delim">
            <!-- "=" not a space: a bare-space value risks being lost on the encoder command line -->
            <value>=</value>
          </input>
          <input into="strings_to_encode">
            <value>ZERO DAY TRAINING LAB - web host. Lab check-in token</value>
            <datastore>scan_flag</datastore>
          </input>
        </encoder>

        <!-- ROT13 price list: decode for flag3 -->
        <encoder module_path="modules/encoders/string/rot13">
          <input into="strings_to_encode">
            <value>FILED: CONFIDENTIAL - STAFF ONLY

ZERO DAY: Q4 2024 PRICE LIST (clients only)

Premium tier:
- ProFTPD 1.3.3c source backdoor: $25,000 (healthcare premium +40% applied)
- Apache Struts2 RCE: $18,000
- Windows SMBv3 RCE: $45,000

Standard tier:
- distcc 2.x remote exec (CVE-2004-2687): $3,200 (legacy estates)
- Assorted SQLi vectors: $800-$2,400

Note: healthcare buyers carry a 40% premium. They pay fast and they pay quietly.

Buyer details are held internally. Ask your account contact.</value>
            <generator type="flag_generator"/>
          </input>
        </encoder>
      </input>
    </service>

    <!-- flag 4: distcc daemon command execution (CVE-2004-2687, high port 3632).
         The recovered transaction log is operational_log_content verbatim
         (scenario.json.erb:61), itemised as m02's invoice ZDS-2024-0847. -->
    <vulnerability module_path="vulnerabilities/unix/misc/distcc_exec">
      <input into="strings_to_leak">
        <value>ZERO DAY TRANSACTION LOG (recovered from the training network)

[2024-10-21 14:32] Invoice ZDS-2024-0847 to GHOST (Ransomware Incorporated), verified
  ProFTPD 1.3.3c source backdoor      $25,000
  Reconnaissance (214 hospitals)      $15,000
  Target Selection Consultation       $10,000
  Deployment Guide                     $5,000
  TOTAL                               $55,000
  15% ENTROPY affiliate discount applied
  Target: St. Catherine's Regional Medical Centre (from the 214-site survey)
  Approved: SABLE / Architect sign-off on file
  Status: DELIVERED
  (Healthcare list pricing carries a built-in premium; see catalogue.)

[2024-11-15 02:47] Deployment confirmed
  Target: St. Catherine's Regional Medical Centre
  Ransom demand: GBP 150,000 (BTC)

[NOTE] Phase 2 sourcing continues.</value>
        <generator type="flag_generator"/>
      </input>
    </vulnerability>

    <vulnerability module_path=".*/ssh_root_login">
      <input into="root_password">
        <datastore>server_root_password</datastore>
      </input>
    </vulnerability>

    <network type="private_network">
      <input into="IP_address">
        <datastore access="1">IP_addresses</datastore>
      </input>
    </network>
    <input into_datastore="spoiler_admin_pass">
      <generator type="strong_password_generator"/>
    </input>
    <build type="cleanup">
      <input into="root_password">
        <datastore>spoiler_admin_pass</datastore>
      </input>
    </build>
  </system>

</scenario>
